<Shell title="Fern & Field — A fictional service-business case | Krespo Labs" description="The design intent, implemented static features, reproduction steps, and limitations of a fictional garden-care website.">
  <div class="case-hero wrap"><a class="text-link" href="/#work">← Back to selected work</a><p class="eyebrow" style="margin-top:40px">01 / FICTIONAL SERVICE-BUSINESS CASE</p><h1>Fern &amp; Field.<br /><em>Rooted in clarity.</em></h1><p class="case-intro">An original garden-care website concept. Designed to make a local service easy to explore, without pretending to be a real business.</p><p class="case-notice">Fictional business · static design demo. No client engagement, customer results, bookings, prices, or real contact details. Enquiries are unavailable and no personal information is collected.</p><a class="button" href="/demos/fern-and-field/">Explore the fictional demo <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
  <div class="case-content wrap"><section><h2>The design problem</h2><p>For this self-directed exercise, the brief was to give a fictional garden-care service a calm, clear home online. The design should explain the service categories, make the process easy to follow, and work on small screens without relying on photography or business claims.</p></section><section><h2>What this draft implements</h2><p>The implementation uses Astro to generate static HTML, with original CSS and SVG garden illustrations. The demo has its own visual identity: warm cream, evergreen, rounded botanical shapes, and a more editorial type treatment than the surrounding portfolio.</p><ul><li>Responsive service descriptions and process sections.</li><li>In-page navigation and native expandable frequently asked questions.</li><li>A prominently labelled fictional-business banner.</li><li>A disabled enquiry control, with an explanation that no request can be sent.</li><li>Semantic page landmarks, a skip link, visible keyboard focus, and reduced-motion support.</li></ul></section><section><h2>Evidence you can inspect</h2><div class="evidence-list"><article><h3>The actual page</h3><p><a href="/demos/fern-and-field/">Open the demo</a>, follow its section links, resize the viewport, and expand the FAQs. This is the working static interface, not an image of one.</p></article><article><h3>The safety boundary</h3><p>There is no form, API endpoint, payment flow, database, or analytics integration. The enquiry button cannot submit anything.</p></article></div></section><section><h2>Reproduce locally</h2><p>From the repository root, run <code>npm ci</code>, then <code>npm run check</code>, <code>npm run build</code>, and <code>npm test</code>. Use <code>npm run preview</code> to inspect the generated site on localhost. Visit <code>/demos/fern-and-field/</code> for the demo.</p><p>Implementation: <code>src/pages/demos/fern-and-field.astro</code> and <code>src/styles/fern-and-field.css</code>. This is a source-review draft, not a deployed service. The owner has approved a public feature branch and pull request; hosting and deployment are not authorized.</p></section><section><h2>Limits, not inflated outcomes</h2><p>This is a portfolio exercise, not a customer project. There are no measured conversion results, reviews, business credentials, or production-service claims. The visuals are original illustrative shapes, not photographs of completed gardens. No booking or enquiry integration is implemented.</p><p>Automated checks are a starting point, not proof of complete accessibility or browser compatibility. Deploying the site or enabling any real enquiry flow would require separate owner approval and an appropriate privacy review.</p></section></div>
</Shell>
